\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{GQA saves cache, not projection FLOPs}
\taxon{Card}
\tag{card}
\meta{anki}{1787063263870}
\meta{anki-deck}{MLSys}

\prompt{Per layer, what do the QKVO projections cost in parameters and training FLOPs under [[XUVV]] with #{N} query heads, #{K} key/value heads, and head dimension #{H}?}{
  \strong{Parameters:} #{2D(N+K)H}, from #{W_Q[D,N,H]} and #{W_O[N,H,D]} at #{DNH} each, plus #{W_K, W_V[D,K,H]} at #{DKH} each.

  \strong{Training FLOPs:} #{12BTD(N+K)H} — each projection is #{6BTD \cdot (\text{heads}) \cdot H}.

  \strong{The variants are one knob.} #{K = N} is plain multi-head attention; #{K = 1} is [MQA](W4YP); #{1 < K < N} with #{K \mid N} is grouped-query, with group size #{G = N/K}.

  \strong{Read the formula for what shrinking #{K} actually buys.} It touches only the #{K} term, so the projection cost falls from #{2N} to #{N + K} — at most a factor of two, and usually far less. That is not why anyone uses GQA. The payoff is the [[TJLA]], which scales with #{K} alone and is the binding constraint at generation time.
}
